}.\", \"{statement}. True or False?\"],\n \"overall\": \"FAIL\",\n \"reasoning\": \"F10 asserts that the ones digit oa is less than ob. Its oa and ob slots are computed from the generated a and b (reason arithmetic), and template_chosen_by_values is empty, so no generated value selects F10. The a and b domains allow pairs where oa is not less than ob (for example a printed 15 with b printed 3), so F10 can print a false comparison. F08 has the same structure: its oa and ob are computed from a and b with no value selecting it, so it can print a subtraction of the ones where the result is negative, which is outside grade 2 scope and outside the cumulative vocabulary. F09 has an appended note slot whose members include the claim that one ten became ten ones and a no-regrouping choice, but the note is not selected by any generated value (template_chosen_by_values is empty), so the regrouping claim can print on problems that did not regroup. The hint families F08, F09 and F10 therefore lack a declared condition, and the packet does not show one. F20 wraps the statement from F19 or F21 in a True or False frame. The F19 statement ends with a period, which F20 follows with another period, and the F21 statement can end with a question (the F04 text ends with What is the missing number?), which F20 follows with a period and True or False? This produces a question presented as a true or false statement and malformed punctuation. Clauses illustrate_subtraction, 2_digit_by_1_digit and number_line are produced by F06, and inverse_of_addition by F05. The arithmetic correctness of the computed slots is left to the hint contract, as the reason_kinds entry states.\"}",
